>>> Since Makeindex is already written in C, it seems obvious that it
>>> should be ported to CWEB.  I hope that nobody thinks that
>>> "Rewrite of Makeindex in WEB" means Pascal WEB.  This would be
>>> a complete waste of time, and a gross misunderstanding of the WEB idea.
>>> I'm probably "running through open doors" on this, but I just wanted
>>> to be sure.

I'm not sure what Dominik's impersonator means by `running through open doors',
but when I read `Rewrite of Makeindex in WEB', I was overjoyed; if something is
written in Pascal-WEB, I can (a) understand it; (b) write or modify change-
files for it; and (c) fix it if it doesn't work (sometimes!).  And, given the
existence of WEB-to-C, those sites that prefer to work and think in `C' can do
the same.  But if something is written in CWEB, it is absolutely useless to me,
for there is no equivalent CWEB-to-Pascal.  Given this distinct asymmetry, is it
not reasonable to regard Pascal-WEB as the standard, and to continue to use it
as the primary distribution medium, using WEB-to-C for those sites or systems
where a decent Pascal compiler does not exist, or where the received wisdom
dictates a preference for `C'?

					Philip Taylor, RHBNC.

